course: everyday
lesson: 498
title: The only child: a quiet house
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An only child is a child with no brothers or sisters. In Dorset, Esme lets herself in at 3:45 and Dad, a postman, is home at 4. For fifteen minutes the house is so quiet she can hear the fridge hum. She puts the radio on «so it's not spooky». She is half proud to be trusted with a key and half creeped out. A short time alone is fine at 9 or 10 if it's short, a grown-up is close by, and there is a plan: who to call, who not to open the door to. If being alone scares you, say so.
explain: Only children often fill the quiet with imagination. On a farm in Donegal, two miles from the nearest kid, Ruairí kicks a ball against the barn wall for an hour, doing the commentary himself: «And Ruairí scores AGAIN, the crowd goes mental!» He's having great craic (Irish for fun), but on wet days it gets lonely. In Aberdeen, Nell has invented two brothers, Rab and Tam, who get blamed for every mess. «It wisnae me, it was Rab» («wisnae» is Scots for «wasn't»). Mum plays along: «Tell Rab tae hoover, then.» Imaginary brothers and friends are common and healthy.
explain: Every kind of family has good bits and hard bits. In Brisbane, Matilda's mate Shaniqua, who shares a room with three sisters, sleeps over: «You've got heaps of room and no one nicks your stuff, you're so lucky!» («heaps» = lots, «nicks» = steals). Matilda says, «Yeah, and no one to talk to after lights out.» Both girls are surprised the other is jealous. The grass always looks greener on the other side. In Cork, someone tells Aisling, «Only child? You must be well spoilt.» «I do the bins, the dishes AND the hoovering, ye eejit (idiot), there's no one else to do them.» «Spoilt only child» is a stereotype: a lazy idea that isn't true of most only children.
explain: Only children often grow up around grown-ups. In Montreal, Jules goes to grown-up dinners and chats easily about hockey and politics, but at recess kids his age seem loud and silly, and he feels left out. Being silly with friends is a skill too, and a team or club helps. In Truro, Cornwall, it's just Tamsin and Mum: «proper job» Sunday pancakes (Cornish for «excellent»), singing in the car. Tamsin calls Mum her best mate, and sometimes worries what Mum would do without her. Being close is lovely, but Mum's feelings are Mum's job; Tamsin's job is being a kid.
explain: In Brooklyn, Desmond comes home with a key on a lanyard, microwaves pasta and does homework at the counter; his mom texts «u good?» and he sends a thumbs-up. He's lowkey bored (a bit, quietly). On holiday in Spain, Kwabena from Croydon is the only kid in his family; Mum and Dad want to read by the pool. By day two he's teamed up with two German kids using hand signals: only children often learn to make friends fast. And in Vancouver, when Hiroshi's mom asks if he wants a brother or sister, he says, «Nah, I'd have to share my Switch.» Then at bedtime: «Would they be younger?» Mixed feelings are normal, and the decision is the parents'.
fact: An only child is a child with no brothers or sisters.
fact: A short time alone after school can be fine at 9 or 10 if it is short, a grown-up is close and there are clear rules.
fact: A good plan for being home alone says who to call and who you may open the door to.
fact: If being alone scares you, it is fine to tell your parents and ask for an after-school club instead.
fact: Only children often invent games, voices and imaginary siblings; this is common and healthy.
fact: Every family set-up has good bits and hard bits; kids from big and small families often envy each other.
fact: «Spoilt only child» is a stereotype; many only children do plenty of chores.
fact: Only children often spend lots of time with adults, so they may talk like adults and find silly play harder at first.
fact: Only children often learn to make friends fast, because they want a playmate.
fact: A child and a parent on their own can be very close, but a parent's feelings are the parent's job.
fact: It is normal to both want and not want a brother or sister; the decision belongs to the parents.
fact: «Craic» is Irish for fun, «wisnae» is Scots for wasn't, «heaps» means lots, «nicks» means steals, «eejit» means idiot and «proper job» is Cornish for excellent.
exercise: What is an only child? | A child with no brothers or sisters | A child who is the oldest of many | A child who lives with one parent | A child who is always alone all day
exercise: Why does Esme put the radio on? | So the quiet isn't spooky | To dance for her dad | To hear the traffic news | Because the fridge broke
exercise: What makes a short time home alone okay at 9 or 10? | It's short, a grown-up is near, and there's a plan | The child is brave enough never to call anyone at all | The doors are left open so neighbours can pop in | The TV is kept on loud the entire time till Dad's back
exercise: What does Ruairí do on the farm? | Kicks a ball at the barn wall and does the commentary | Sits indoors watching football on his phone all day | Waits by the gate for a kid two miles away to pass | Milks the cows on his own every single afternoon
exercise: Who are Rab and Tam? | Nell's made-up brothers | Nell's real cousins | Nell's two dogs | The boys next door
exercise: What does Nell's mum say about Rab? | «Tell Rab tae hoover, then» | «Stop lying, Rab isn't real, Nell» | «Rab is grounded for a whole week» | «Who on earth is Rab, then, hen?»
exercise: Shaniqua says Matilda is lucky. What does Matilda miss? | Someone to talk to after lights out | Sharing her stuff with heaps of people | Having less room for her own toys | A sister who nicks all her clothes
exercise: «No one nicks your stuff.» What does «nicks» mean? | Steals | Cuts | Names | Fixes
exercise: What does Aisling say when someone calls her spoilt? | She does the bins, dishes and hoovering herself | She agrees and lists all her expensive presents | She cries and says she wishes she had a brother | She says only children never have to help out
exercise: Why can Jules talk easily with adults? | He spends lots of time with them | He is secretly a grown-up | He hates other children | His teacher made him
exercise: What can help Jules get on with kids his age? | A team or a club | Talking about politics more loudly | Staying at grown-up dinners only | Avoiding recess every day
exercise: Tamsin worries what Mum would do without her. What does the lesson say? | Mum's feelings are Mum's job; Tamsin's is being a kid | Tamsin must never leave Mum's side, not even for school | Tamsin should look after Mum's feelings every day | Mum would be fine, so Tamsin should stop being so close
exercise: How does Desmond tell his mom he's fine? | Sends a thumbs-up | Calls her every minute | Writes a long letter | Goes to her office
exercise: How does Kwabena make friends on holiday? | Hand signals with two German kids | By reading by the pool with Mum and Dad | He waits until the holiday is over | He only plays with grown-ups at the bar
exercise: Hiroshi says «Nah» to a sibling, then asks «Would they be younger?» What does that show? | Mixed feelings are normal | He's lying about the Switch | He definitely wants twins | He doesn't care at all
exercise: Whose decision is having another baby? | The parents' | The child's | The school's | The grandparents'
test: «only kids are all spoilt brats lol». What's true? | It's a stereotype; many do loads of chores | Yes, every only child is spoilt rotten | Only kids never have to do any chores | Only kids get double pocket money by law
test: «my lil sis made up a friend called Bobo, is she weird??». What does the lesson say? | Imaginary friends are common and healthy | She needs to see a doctor straight away | It means she's lonely and sad for ever | Only babies ever have imaginary friends
test: Esme's dad is stuck in traffic and someone she doesn't know knocks at the door. What should her plan say? | Don't open; call Dad or the grown-up on the list | Open the door and ask what they want politely | Hide in the garden shed until it's dark | Open it but keep the chain on and chat
test: «Matilda's got her own room, bet she's never jealous of anyone». What happened at the sleepover? | Each girl was jealous of the other | Matilda was happy with no one to talk to | Shaniqua wished she had more sisters | Neither of them noticed anything
test: What would probably happen if Jules joined a hockey team? | He'd get practice being silly with kids his age | He'd stop being able to talk to any adults ever | He'd have to give up all his grown-up dinners | Nothing; teams don't help anyone make friends
test: «Tamsin should look after her mum's feelings, she's all Mum has». What does the lesson say? | Mum's feelings are Mum's job | Yes, Tamsin is in charge of Mum | Tamsin should stay home always | Tamsin must never have mates
test: A cartoon shows an only child who never makes a friend all summer. What does Kwabena's holiday show instead? | Only children often learn to make friends fast | Only children can't talk to kids from other countries | Only children always prefer reading by the pool | Only children need their parents to find them friends
test: «Desmond's home alone after school, that's dodgy, someone should report it». What's the fair view? | A short time with clear rules and a grown-up on call can be fine | Any child alone for a single minute must be reported to the police | It's always fine for kids to be alone all evening with no rules | Kids should never have a key to their own front door, ever
test: ★★★ Esme gets in at 3:45, Dad at 4. On Fridays Dad is 10 minutes late. How long is she alone on a Friday? | 25 minutes | 15 minutes | 10 minutes | 35 minutes
test: ★★★ Nell blames Rab for 3 messes a day and Tam for 2. Mum says, «Rab and Tam can tidy half the messes each day.» In 2 days, how many messes do Rab and Tam tidy? | 5 | 10 | 4 | 6
test: «Hiroshi said nah to a brother so he defo doesn't want one». What's wrong with that? | He has mixed feelings, which is normal | He's wanted a brother since he was born | He actually wants a dog, not a brother | He's never thought about it at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the proposition that only children are invariably spoilt? | It is a stereotype unsupported by the circumstances of most only children | It is an established truth confirmed by all the evidence | It applies to every child possessing a room of his or her own | It is correct save where the child resides upon a farm
exam: Identify the conditions under which a brief period alone at home may be appropriate for a child aged nine or ten. | Brevity, a nearby adult and a clear plan | An open door so that any caller may enter freely | The absence of any rules or telephone numbers | A period extending until late in the evening
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning imaginary siblings invented by an only child. | They are common and healthy | They indicate a grave disorder requiring treatment | They are confined to children under three years of age | They signify that the child is unloved by its parents
exam: Whereas a child expresses both reluctance and curiosity regarding a prospective sibling, state the correct characterisation of such feelings. | Mixed feelings of this kind are normal | They demonstrate that the child is untruthful | They indicate the child wishes to be rid of the parents | They oblige the parents to defer to the child's decision
